/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_BuildGuide_Windows
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildWindowsToolchains Supported Windows Toolchains
 *
 * Windows precompiled libraries must be separated by toolchain.
 *
 * Supported target presets:
 *
 * @code
 * windows_msvc_x64
 * windows_clang_x64
 * @endcode
 *
 * `windows_msvc_x64` selects the `Visual Studio 18 2026` multi-configuration
 * generator and x64 architecture. `windows_clang_x64` selects `clang`/`clang++`
 * with Ninja Multi-Config and validates that the host processor is x86-64.
 * Their archives are separate compatibility products even when both use `.lib`.
 *
 * Optional future presets:
 *
 * @code
 * windows_mingw_x64
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildWindowsMSVC MSVC
 *
 * MSVC uses a multi-configuration generator.
 *
 * CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is ignored by Visual Studio generators.
 * Always pass the configuration during build and install.
 *
 * Run from Visual Studio Developer PowerShell so MSVC, the Windows SDK, and
 * environment variables refer to the same installation.
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * Get-Command cmake
 * Get-Command cl
 *
 * ./scripts/configure.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Fresh
 * ./scripts/build.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 `
 *     -Configuration Debug -Target Foundation
 * ./scripts/build.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 `
 *     -Configuration Release -Target Foundation
 * @endcode
 *
 * For Clang, verify `clang++` and `ninja`, then replace the preset name with
 * `windows_clang_x64` in the same workflow.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildWindowsApplication Application Development
 *
 * A Windows application should consume Foundation through the `FetchContent`
 * workflow in @ref Foundation_BuildGuide_Overview. Configure that application
 * with its own chosen compiler; no Foundation export or `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` is
 * needed for Debug or Release development. For example, from Developer
 * PowerShell with MSVC:
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * cmake -S . -B build -G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64
 * cmake --build build --config Debug
 * @endcode
 *
 * Use the same application workflow with its Clang toolchain when targeting
 * `windows_clang_x64`. Foundation is then compiled by that application build,
 * so MSVC and Clang artifacts are never mixed.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildWindowsExport Export
 *
 * Recommended:
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Fresh
 * @endcode
 *
 * The export command builds and installs a Release-only package. Expect
 * `Foundation.lib`, public headers, build metadata, and Release imported-target
 * files below `dist/windows_msvc_x64`. `Foundation_d.lib` remains available only
 * in the source build tree.
 * Add `-ExamplesOn` only when the package should include Release API examples.
 * The package then includes one `FoundationExamples.cmd` menu launcher that
 * asks after every selected example whether to return to the menu or exit.
 * See @ref Foundation_Workflows_Manual for the complete local sequence and the
 * distinction between incremental and clean configuration.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildWindowsTests Test Status
 *
 * Native Windows runs the registered CTest suite. Execute all discovered
 * GoogleTest cases in both Debug and Release for `windows_msvc_x64` and
 * `windows_clang_x64`; a zero-test result is not release evidence. Use a fresh
 * export afterward so unrelated cached feature overrides do not leak into the
 * release package.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildWindowsValidation Validation
 *
 * Inspect MSVC output with `dumpbin /headers` and Clang output with
 * `llvm-readobj` where available. The Release-package validation intentionally
 * compiles a separate x64 consumer using `find_package(Foundation CONFIG
 * REQUIRED)` and verifies it selects `Foundation.lib`. That check validates a
 * distributed binary; application development uses `FetchContent` above. The
 * complete command checklist is in @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation.
 */
